From f9b72e4ffbb1fc5365226066f2f632dd0f320fdc Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 08:20:25 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] windowing/gbm: what the display is sent is read again where
 the id of the blob of HDR metadata comes back

CHDROutputRead reads the EOTF of the blob of HDR_OUTPUT_METADATA once for
each id, on the ground that an id names a blob that never changes, and
kept the id read last also while the property was empty. An id names the
same bytes only while its blob lives: the kernel gives each new mode
object, blobs among them, the lowest id that is free (idr_alloc() in
__drm_mode_object_add(), drm_mode_object.c), so the blob of the HDR
metadata of the next video can have the id of the one of the video
before, which Kodi destroyed when that video ended. After an HDR10 video,
the menus and then an HLG video, Player.Process(videooutputhdrtype) and
(videooutputeotf) could say HDR10 and PQ for the HLG video, and the other
way round.

The id read last is forgotten when the property is found empty or not
there (between two videos, where SettleHDR() and the player information
read it), and when the blob of another id cannot be read. The same id
while it stays on the property is still read once. The host test of the
reader had a check that asked for the id to be kept across an empty
property; with the reuse of an id it is read again now, and a check of
that is added (a copy of the test, changed so, fails without this and
passes with it).
---
 xbmc/windowing/gbm/HDROutputRead.h | 28 +++++++++++++++++++---------
 1 file changed, 19 insertions(+), 9 deletions(-)

diff --git a/xbmc/windowing/gbm/HDROutputRead.h b/xbmc/windowing/gbm/HDROutputRead.h
index d461747..13ad686 100644
--- a/xbmc/windowing/gbm/HDROutputRead.h
+++ b/xbmc/windowing/gbm/HDROutputRead.h
@@ -29,9 +29,12 @@ namespace GBM
  * something else (a commit that it refused, metadata that another request carried). The truth for
  * a skin, an add-on or a person is the blob of the connector's HDR_OUTPUT_METADATA property: its
  * `hdmi_metadata_type1.eotf` (0 traditional gamma SDR, 1 traditional gamma HDR, 2 PQ, 3 HLG,
- * CTA-861-G). It is read once for each id of the blob (an id names a blob that never changes), not
- * for each frame, and the labels that say what the display is sent (Player.Process(
- * videooutputhdrtype, videooutputeotf)) come from that reading.
+ * CTA-861-G). It is read once for each id of the blob, not for each frame, and the labels that say
+ * what the display is sent (Player.Process(videooutputhdrtype, videooutputeotf)) come from that
+ * reading. An id names the same bytes only as long as its blob lives: the kernel gives the id of a
+ * blob that is gone to the next object it makes (idr_alloc() of drm_mode_object.c, the lowest free
+ * one), so the id read last is forgotten when the property is found empty, as it is between two
+ * videos (the next blob of HDR metadata may have that id with other bytes).
  *
  * Nothing of DRM is here: the layout of struct hdr_output_metadata (include/uapi/drm/drm_mode.h,
  * 32 bytes: a u32 metadata_type, then struct hdr_metadata_infoframe, whose first byte is the EOTF)
@@ -133,24 +136,31 @@ public:
 
   /*!
    * \brief The reading of the blob for an id: the blob is read (with \p readBlob, which gives the
-   *        EOTF of the blob of an id) only when the id is not the one read last
+   *        EOTF of the blob of an id) only when the id is not the one read last, and that one is
+   *        forgotten when the property is found empty (or not there): its blob may be gone, and
+   *        its id given to another
    * \param id the value of HDR_OUTPUT_METADATA; none where the connector has no such property
    */
   template<typename ReadBlob>
   Eotf Get(std::optional<uint64_t> id, ReadBlob readBlob)
   {
-    if (!id)
-      return Eotf::NO_PROPERTY;
-    if (*id == 0)
-      return Eotf::NO_BLOB;
     std::lock_guard lock(m_lock);
+    if (!id || *id == 0)
+    {
+      m_id.reset();
+      return id ? Eotf::NO_BLOB : Eotf::NO_PROPERTY;
+    }
     if (!m_id || *m_id != *id)
     {
       const Eotf read = readBlob(*id);
       ++m_reads;
-      // (a blob that could not be read is asked for again: it may be a call that failed)
+      // (a blob that could not be read is asked for again: it may be a call that failed; the id
+      // read before is not on the property any more)
       if (read == Eotf::UNREADABLE)
+      {
+        m_id.reset();
         return read;
+      }
       m_eotf = read;
       m_id = *id;
     }
-- 
